    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Treadmill calorie readouts are estimates, and in practice they tend to run high —
          commonly by something like 10 to 20%, and by more if you hold the handrails or never
          entered your weight. The console calculates energy use from belt speed, incline,
          time and a body weight using standard equations, and those equations describe an
          average person walking or running without support. You are not average, and you may
          not be unsupported.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Are treadmill calories accurate?</strong> Roughly, at best. A treadmill
            cannot measure the energy you use. It estimates it, using your speed, the incline,
            the time elapsed and a body weight — either one you entered or a default programmed
            into the console. The estimate is built on reasonable science, but it is an average
            applied to an individual, and several common habits push it upward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How many calories do you burn on a treadmill?</strong> For a rough sense of
            scale: a 150 lb person walking at 3 mph on the flat uses somewhere in the region of
            120 calories in 30 minutes, and the same person running at 6 mph uses something like
            330. A 200 lb person uses roughly a third more in both cases. The
            <a href="#calorie-table" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">table below</a> gives
            estimates at several speeds and gradients, with the working shown.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to do with the number.</strong> Use it to compare sessions on the same
            machine — a higher figure does reflect more work. Do not treat it as a precise
            accounting of calories you have earned, and do not compare the readout on one brand
            of treadmill with the readout on another, because the assumptions behind them differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-consoles-calculate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How a Treadmill Console Calculates Calories</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmills use some version of the metabolic equations published by the
            American College of Sports Medicine, or an equivalent approach based on METs. Both
            start from the same idea: walking and running at a given speed and gradient cost a
            predictable amount of oxygen per kilogram of body weight, and oxygen consumption
            converts to energy at roughly five calories per litre.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ACSM walking equation, for example, estimates oxygen use from three terms: one
            for horizontal speed, one for vertical work against the incline, and a constant for
            resting metabolism. The running equation has the same structure with different
            coefficients, because running costs roughly twice as much oxygen per metre of
            horizontal travel as walking. Multiply the oxygen figure by body weight and time, and
            you have a calorie estimate.
          </p>
          <p>
            A MET — metabolic equivalent of task — is the same idea in a simpler unit. One MET is
            the energy cost of sitting quietly; an activity rated at 4 METs costs about four times
            that. Researchers have catalogued MET values for hundreds of activities in the
            Compendium of Physical Activities, and the rough calculation is:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>calories ≈ METs × body weight in kg × hours</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Either way, the inputs are speed, gradient, time and weight. Nothing about your
            fitness, your efficiency, your age, your body composition or whether you are holding
            on enters the calculation — and those are exactly the things that make you different
            from the average. Some manufacturers add age, sex or heart rate to their formula, but
            the details are rarely published, and you should not assume your console does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-they-overestimate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why Treadmill Calorie Counts Usually Run High</h2>
<p>
            Small studies and laboratory comparisons commonly cited on this subject tend to find
            treadmills overestimating energy expenditure, with figures somewhere around 10 to 20%
            frequently quoted and larger errors in some conditions. The exact number depends on
            the machine, the person and how they use it, so treat any single percentage with
            caution. The direction, though, is consistent, and the reasons are well understood.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Holding the handrails.</strong> This is the largest single source of error
            for most people. The equations assume you are carrying your full body weight up the
            gradient. Grip the front rail or lean on the side rails and some of that weight goes
            through your arms into the frame, and on a steep incline the reduction in effort can
            be substantial. The console has no way of knowing, and it keeps counting as if you
            were unsupported.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The wrong body weight.</strong> If you never enter your weight, the console
            uses a default figure, which varies by manufacturer and is rarely stated
            prominently. Weight scales the estimate almost linearly, so a lighter person using
            the default is overcredited and a heavier person is undercredited. Many users enter
            their weight once and never update it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gross rather than net calories.</strong> Most console figures include the
            energy you would have used anyway, simply being alive, during that time. That
            baseline is roughly one MET — around 35 calories per half-hour for a 150 lb person.
            It is not wrong to include it, but it means the readout is larger than the extra
            energy the session actually cost compared with sitting on the sofa.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Efficiency and fitness.</strong> Experienced walkers and runners tend to move
            more economically than the population average the equations describe, so they use
            somewhat less energy at a given speed. A beginner may use a little more. Neither is
            captured.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt and calibration.</strong> A treadmill whose belt actually runs a little
            slower than the display claims, or whose incline is not quite what it reads, will
            produce a calorie figure based on numbers that are already off. Consumer machines are
            not calibrated the way lab equipment is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calorie-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Many Calories Do You Burn on a Treadmill? Estimates per 30 Minutes</h2>
<p>
            The figures below are calculated, not measured. They use MET values from the
            Compendium of Physical Activities (the widely used 2011 edition; a 2024 update revised
            some entries, so treat these as approximate), and the formula
            <strong>calories = METs × body weight in kg × 0.5 hours</strong>. A 150 lb person is
            68 kg; a 200 lb person is 90.7 kg. These are gross figures, including resting
            metabolism, which is what most consoles show. Individual results can easily differ by
            10 to 20% either way.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking 2.0 mph, level (2.8 METs):</strong> about 95 calories at 150 lb;
            about 125 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking 2.5 mph, level (3.0 METs):</strong> about 100 at 150 lb; about
            135 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking 3.0 mph, level (3.5 METs):</strong> about 120 at 150 lb; about
            160 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking 3.5 mph, level (4.3 METs):</strong> about 145 at 150 lb; about
            195 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking 4.0 mph, level (5.0 METs):</strong> about 170 at 150 lb; about
            225 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking 3.5 mph, 1–5% incline (5.3 METs):</strong> about 180 at 150 lb;
            about 240 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking roughly 3–3.5 mph, 6–15% incline (8.0 METs):</strong> about 270
            at 150 lb; about 365 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Running 5.0 mph, 12:00 per mile (8.3 METs):</strong> about 280 at 150 lb;
            about 375 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Running 6.0 mph, 10:00 per mile (9.8 METs):</strong> about 335 at 150 lb;
            about 445 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Running 7.0 mph, about 8:34 per mile (11.0 METs):</strong> about 375 at
            150 lb; about 500 at 200 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Running 8.0 mph, 7:30 per mile (11.8 METs):</strong> about 400 at 150 lb;
            about 535 at 200 lb.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, body weight scales everything: a 200 lb person uses
            about a third more than a 150 lb person at the same speed, simply because there is
            more mass to move. Second, incline is powerful. Walking briskly up a steep grade
            costs about as much as an easy run, which is why incline walking has become so
            popular — our guide to
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the 12-3-30 workout</a>
            looks at one well-known version.
          </p>
          <p>
            To get the net cost — the extra energy compared with sitting still — subtract
            roughly 35 calories per half-hour at 150 lb, or 45 at 200 lb. For speeds not listed,
            our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            converts mph to minutes per mile.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-calories-burned/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calories burned</a> reference expands this into estimate tables by speed, incline and body weight, with the arithmetic shown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-vs-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Walking vs Running: Per Minute and Per Mile</h2>
<p>
            Running uses considerably more energy per minute than walking, which the table makes
            obvious. Per mile, the gap is much smaller than most people assume.
          </p>
          <p>
            Take the 150 lb person. Walking at 3 mph for 30 minutes covers 1.5 miles for about
            120 calories — around 80 per mile. Running at 6 mph for 30 minutes covers 3 miles for
            about 335 — around 110 per mile. So running costs more per mile, but nowhere near
            three times as much; most of the difference in a 30-minute session is that you cover
            twice the distance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that the activity you will actually repeat matters more
            than its rate per minute. A long walk you take five days a week will add up to more
            than a run you dread and skip. If running is what you want to build toward, the
            <a href="/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">couch to 5k treadmill plan</a>
            is a sensible way to get there without injury.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heart-rate-estimates" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Are Heart-Rate-Based Estimates Better?</h2>
<p>
            Some treadmills, and most fitness trackers, use heart rate to estimate calories
            instead of, or as well as, speed and incline. In principle that adds individual
            information: a fitter person has a lower heart rate at a given workload, and heart
            rate rises with effort in a broadly predictable way.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, it introduces its own errors. The relationship between heart rate and
            energy use differs from person to person, and the device has to assume an average
            relationship unless it has been calibrated against laboratory testing. Heart rate is
            also affected by heat, hydration, caffeine, sleep, stress and medication — beta
            blockers in particular make heart-rate-based estimates unreliable. And on a treadmill,
            the grip sensors on the handrails are the least accurate way to measure heart rate,
            and using them requires holding the rails, which itself reduces the effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            A chest strap gives a much better heart rate reading than grip sensors. It does not,
            on its own, make the calorie estimate accurate. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a>
            covers what heart rate is genuinely useful for, which is gauging intensity rather
            than counting calories.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wearables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Are Fitness Trackers and Smartwatches More Accurate?</h2>
<p>
            Not reliably. Wrist-worn trackers are generally reasonable at measuring heart rate
            during steady exercise and noticeably worse at estimating energy expenditure. A
            widely reported 2017 Stanford-led study of seven wrist devices found that none
            estimated energy expenditure to within 20% on average, with some far worse, and later
            research has continued to find calorie estimates the weakest feature of consumer
            wearables.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is not a criticism of the technology so much as a reflection of the problem.
            Measuring energy expenditure properly requires either analysing the oxygen and carbon
            dioxide you breathe, or specialised laboratory methods. Everything else — console,
            watch, app — is an estimate built on averages.
          </p>
          <p>
            So if your watch and your treadmill disagree, neither is necessarily right. A sensible
            approach is to pick one source, use it consistently, and pay attention to the trend
            rather than to any individual figure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="more-accurate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How to Get a More Realistic Number</h2>
<p>
            You cannot make a console accurate, but you can remove the errors that are under
            your control.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Enter your current weight.</strong> If your treadmill asks for it, give it,
            and update it when it changes. This is the single biggest improvement for most
            people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Let go of the handrails.</strong> If you need to hold on to manage a speed or
            incline, the setting is higher than you can actually sustain, and the console is
            crediting you for work your arms are partly avoiding. Turn it down and walk
            unsupported. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a>
            covers when holding on is and is not the right call.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mentally discount the figure.</strong> If you want a working number, knocking
            10 to 20% off the readout is a reasonable rule of thumb, and more if you know you
            have been leaning on the rails. It is still an estimate, but a more honest one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Compare like with like.</strong> The readout is most useful as a relative
            measure on the same machine. If this week's 30-minute session shows a higher number
            than last month's at the same weight, you did more work. That comparison holds even
            if the absolute figure is generous.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-loss-context" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What This Means if You Are Exercising to Lose Weight</h2>
<p>
            This is where the overestimate matters most. A generous calorie readout, combined with
            the natural tendency to reward a workout with food, can quietly cancel out the energy
            a session actually cost. Treating the console number as calories "earned" is one of
            the most common reasons people are surprised that their weight has not changed.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also worth knowing that the body partially compensates for exercise — through
            appetite, and through moving a bit less during the rest of the day — so the effect on
            body weight is usually smaller than a straight calorie sum would suggest. None of
            that makes exercise less worth doing. Its benefits for cardiovascular fitness, blood
            pressure, sleep and mood arrive whether or not the scale moves.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts for weight loss</a>
            is honest about what a treadmill can and cannot do here, and our answer to
            <a href="/does-treadmill-burn-belly-fat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill burns belly fat</a>
            deals with the spot-reduction question. For anything involving diet,
            calorie targets or a medical condition, a registered dietitian or your doctor is the
            right person to ask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="machine-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Does the Treadmill Itself Make a Difference?</h2>
<p>
            Only at the margins. A more expensive treadmill does not burn more calories at the
            same speed and gradient; the physics of moving your body is the same. What a better
            machine may offer is a more consistent belt speed under load, a more accurate incline
            reading, a console that lets you enter weight and age, and support for a chest-strap
            heart rate monitor. Those help the estimate be a little less wrong, not right.
          </p>
          <p>
            What genuinely changes how much energy you use is incline range and how willing you
            are to use it. If calorie expenditure is a priority, a machine with a generous
            powered incline is more useful than one with a sophisticated console — our guide to
            the <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>
            covers machines built around that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Bottom Line on Treadmill Calories</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Treadmill calorie counts are estimates, and they usually run high</strong> —
            by around 10 to 20% in commonly cited comparisons, and more if you hold the rails or
            have not entered your weight. They are calculated from speed, incline, time and body
            weight using standard equations that describe an average person.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Realistic figures</strong> for 30 minutes run from roughly 95 to 170 calories
            walking on the flat at 150 lb, to around 335 running at 6 mph, with a 200 lb person
            using about a third more and steep incline walking approaching running costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the number to compare sessions, not to count intake.</strong> Enter your
            weight, let go of the rails, discount the readout a little, and remember that the most
            valuable thing about a treadmill session is that you did it — and will do it again.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmill calories accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not very. Treadmills estimate calories from speed, incline, time and a body weight, using equations based on an average person, and they commonly overestimate — figures around 10 to 20% are often cited, with larger errors if you hold the handrails or never entered your weight. Use the readout to compare sessions on the same machine rather than as an exact count.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many calories do you burn on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly, from Compendium of Physical Activities MET values: a 150 lb person burns about 120 calories walking 30 minutes at 3 mph and about 335 running 30 minutes at 6 mph. A 200 lb person burns about 160 and 445 respectively. Brisk walking on a steep incline can approach the cost of an easy run. These are estimates, and individuals vary.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmills overestimate calories?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. The main reasons are holding the handrails, which takes weight off your legs while the console keeps counting as if it did not; a default body weight that does not match yours; figures that include the calories you would have burned at rest anyway; and equations that assume average efficiency rather than yours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does holding the handrails reduce calories burned on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The calorie equations assume you are carrying your full body weight, especially up an incline. Leaning on or gripping the rails transfers part of that weight to the frame, which reduces the work your legs do, while the console continues to count as if you were unsupported. If you need to hold on to keep up, lower the speed or incline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is my smartwatch more accurate than the treadmill for calories?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. Wrist trackers are reasonable at heart rate during steady exercise but considerably weaker at estimating energy expenditure; a widely reported 2017 Stanford-led study found none of seven devices was within 20% on average. Pick one source, use it consistently, and watch the trend rather than the absolute number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I subtract calories from the treadmill readout?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Knocking roughly 10 to 20% off is a reasonable rule of thumb if you want a working figure, and more if you have been holding the rails. Also bear in mind the readout normally includes resting metabolism — around 35 calories per half-hour for a 150 lb person — so the extra cost of the session is a little lower still.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Calorie Counts</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The readout is not the problem so much as how it gets used. These four habits turn a rough estimate into a misleading one.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never entering your weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Consoles fall back on a default weight when you skip that step, and the estimate scales almost directly with weight. A lighter person is overcredited and a heavier person undercredited. Enter your current weight and update it when it changes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaning on the rails on a steep incline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">This can remove a large share of the effort while the console keeps counting as if you were carrying your whole body up the hill. It is the biggest single source of inflated readouts. Lower the incline until you can walk unsupported.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the readout as calories earned</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Eating back a generous console figure is one of the most common reasons exercise appears not to work for weight. Use the number to compare sessions with each other, not as a budget for food — and take diet questions to a registered dietitian.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing numbers across machines and devices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Different treadmills, gym machines and watches use different formulas and assumptions, so their calorie figures are not comparable. A higher number at the gym than at home does not mean you worked harder. Stick to one source and watch its trend.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

